TASK 1 – TRAFFIC SIZING

Robot controllers:
40 × 200 bytes / 0.002 s × 8 = 32.00 Mbps

Vibration sensors:
600 × 50 bytes/s × 8 = 0.24 Mbps

CCTV:
24 × 4 Mbps = 96.00 Mbps

Office:
150 × 10% active × 2 Mbps = 30.00 Mbps

Total per line:
32 + 0.24 + 96 + 30 = 158.24 Mbps

Eight lines:
8 × 158.24 = 1,265.92 Mbps = 1.26592 Gbps

Conclusion:
A single 1 Gbps uplink is NOT sufficient for eight lines. It is oversubscribed by
265.92 Mbps before additional protocol overhead/bursts.

Most data:
CCTV = 96 Mbps.

Strictest delay requirement:
Robot commands, because the scenario gives a 10 ms deadline.

They are NOT the same: CCTV has the highest volume; robot commands have the strictest timing.

100 ms delay harm ranking:
1. Robot commands – violates the 10 ms deadline and can affect control.
2. AGV/industrial control traffic – real-time movement/control can become unsafe.
3. CCTV – delay affects live viewing but usually does not stop control.
4. Office/ERP/e-mail – delay is inconvenient but generally tolerable.

Alternative: CCTV sends motion clips only 5% of the time:
CCTV = 96 × 0.05 = 4.80 Mbps
New line total = 32 + 0.24 + 4.80 + 30 = 67.04 Mbps
Eight lines = 536.32 Mbps

Therefore a 1 Gbps uplink is enough for eight lines under the motion-triggered CCTV assumption.
